LMSCoverDisplay (or just LMSDisplay) is code to display cover art for a currently playing song/album on a Lyrion Music Server player.
Inspired by TuneShine, and a couple of other projects.

Really, I saw the ads for TuneShine, and said "I can do that", so instead of buying something for $200, I probably spent at least as much, and hours
of time building my own version.

Hardware:
This code is written for a Raspberry Pi Zero2 W, with an Adafruit RGB Matrix Bonnet, and an Adafruit 64x64 RGB Display.
It should work with other hardware configurations that are supported by the Raspberry Pi RGB Matrix Driver, but you're on your own.

Also supported is an optional remote control.   This is based on a Waveshare MCP23017 sitting on the i2c bus, with multiple buttons attached
for skip ahead, back, play/pause, volume up, and volume down.   This should work with any MCP23017 i2c adapter, but the in assignments will
need to be edited appropriately.

Waveshare MCP23017: https://www.amazon.com/dp/B07P2H1NZG?th=1
Adafruit RGB Matrix Bonnet: https://www.adafruit.com/product/3211 or https://www.digikey.com/en/products/detail/adafruit-industries-llc/3211/8535237?s=N4IgTCBcDaIIYBM4DMBOBXAlgFwASoHMAjXAWzm1UwA9ciB7AO0YFNsQBdAXyA
Raspbery Pi Zero 2w: wherever you can find them.   Seem to be scare at the time of writing.
Display: https://www.adafruit.com/product/3649 or https://www.adafruit.com/product/5362 or https://www.adafruit.com/product/4732 depending on how big you want it to be.

Hardware Preparation:
Prepare the matrix bonnet per instructions here: https://learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-rgb-matrix-bonnet-for-raspberry-pi/matrix-setup
Use both the "Configure for 64x64 matrix" (short Jumper E to pin 8), and "Configure for Quality/Convienenc" (connect a wire from Pin 4 to pin 18)

A note on display drivers.
  LMSDisplay supports running the display thorugh either the rgb-matrix-driver or a flasch-taschen server.   The former
  is preferred, but it cannot be built on the Pi Zero 2W.   It seems to run out of memory during the build process, and crashes 
  the system.   You can build it on a larger system, for example a Raspberry PI 4 or 5, you just need to make sure 
  you are running the same OS on each, at the point in time where I wrote this, I was using a 64 bit version of Raspbian Trixie.

  If you don't have access to another machine, you can use the Flaschen-Taschen driver.   This will build on a Raspberry PI 2.   flaschen-taschen
  is a network server that sits on UDP port 1337, and displays images sent to it.

Building the Flaschen-Taschen server:
  - git clone https://github.com/hzeller/flaschen-taschen
  - pip install flaschen-taschen/api/python
  - cd flaschen-taschen/server
  - make FT_BACKEND=rgb-matrix
  - sudo cp ft-server /usr/local/bin

Building the rpi rgb-matrix-driver
  On a more powerful machine, running the same OS as the Pi Zero 2W, execute the following command.
    pip wheel git+https://github.com/hzeller/rpi-rgb-led-matrix
  This will generate a file with a name like:
    rgbmatrix-0.0.1+20260125-cp313-cp313-linux_aarch64.whl
  Copy this file to the Pi Zero 2w and install it there
    - sudo pip install rgbmatrix-0.0.1+20260125-cp313-cp313-linux_aarch64.whl

Gather the software:
Get the following github projects:
  - https://github.com/koldinger/LMSCoverDisplay/release/latest
  - https://github.com/koldinger/LMSTools/release/latest
  - https://github.com/koldinger/WifiSelect/release/latest

I like to build them into wheels, and install from them.  Personally, I use "uv", but your favorite way will work.   For example:
  - git clone https://github.com/koldinger/LMSCoverDisplay
  - cd LMSCoverDisplay
  - uv build
Repeat for each project.

Copy the ft-server file from the first step, the dist/*.whl files from each project, and the install.sh and *.service files from LMSCoverDisplay
to your target machine.

Run the install.sh script.
  - sudo bash -x install.sh 

Install the python projects:
  - sudo pip install wifiselect-XXXX.whl
  - sudo pip install lmstools-XXXX.whl
  - sudo pip install lmsdisplay-XXXX.whl

XXXX will typically be a version number and some other info, so something like 0.5.5-py3-none-any.
XXXX will probably be different for each wheel.

Once this is done, you should be able to reboot.

If you plan on using the flaschen-taschen server, enable the ft-server service
  - sudo systemctl enable ft-server.service

---- Running
When everything is installed, you can reboot the box, and the service should just come up.

At first, if your display server is not connected to a wifi network, it will show a QRCode.  Scan the QR code
and it will take you to a wifi hotspot you can use to select the wifinetwork that your LMS server is on.

Once you're connected, it should show another QR code to go to a configuration page.    The page is on port
5000 on your box, so it should have a URL like:
  http://<ip address of your display>:500

Go to this webpage, and configure the platform.   Be sure to select a player to watch, and any options you might like.
If you are using the Flaschen-Taschen server, be sure to select the Flaschen driver under the advanced options button.

----- Note:
On the configuration page, the Update button does not yet work.   Next release, I hope.

